1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:00,962
TL;DR.

2
00:00:00,962 --> 00:00:03,087
This week in 30 seconds:.

3
00:00:03,087 --> 00:00:18,275
Grok 4.5: xAI launches its first model since going public and acquiring Cursor, at $2/$6 per million tokens — significantly cheaper than Anthropic's Opus 4.7 ($5/$25).

4
00:00:18,275 --> 00:00:26,625
China: Beijing consults with Alibaba, ByteDance, and Z.ai on a tiered export control system for frontier models.

5
00:00:26,625 --> 00:00:37,637
Layoffs: 120,000 tech jobs already cut in 2026, with AI the most-cited reason in May — despite record profits at Oracle and others.

6
00:00:37,637 --> 00:00:48,475
Capital question: The industry is planning $1.5 trillion in 2026 CapEx, but analysts say it needs $3 trillion in revenue to justify it.

7
00:00:48,475 --> 00:00:49,725
Story of the Week.

8
00:00:49,725 --> 00:00:54,637
Grok 4.5: xAI Pivots from Chatbot to Enterprise Tool.

9
00:00:54,637 --> 00:01:07,862
$2 per million input tokens, versus $5 for Anthropic's Opus 4.7 — with this pricing, xAI sends its first model since its own IPO and the Cursor acquisition into the race .

10
00:01:07,862 --> 00:01:23,650
Grok 4.5 launched on July 8, 2026, exactly one week ahead of OpenAI's GPT-5.6 launch announced for July 15, which had previously been temporarily blocked by the Trump administration over safety concerns .

11
00:01:23,650 --> 00:01:30,387
The focus is now explicitly on coding, office work, and agentic tasks rather than the consumer chatbot .

12
00:01:30,387 --> 00:01:35,662
xAI markets the model as "twice as token-efficient" compared to the competition .

13
00:01:35,662 --> 00:01:48,462
On cost, Grok 4.5 significantly undercuts both Opus 4.7 ($5/$25 per million tokens) and OpenAI's Sol model ($5/$30) .

14
00:01:48,462 --> 00:01:54,437
EU users, however, will need to wait until mid-July before the model becomes available there .

15
00:01:54,437 --> 00:02:03,537
"An Opus-class model, but faster, more token-efficient and lower cost — roughly comparable to Opus 4.7, but much faster."

16
00:02:03,537 --> 00:02:04,612
— Elon Musk

17
00:02:04,612 --> 00:02:19,912
For developer and ops teams, this means: anyone who has been hesitant about coding agents due to cost now has a noticeably cheaper alternative at a comparable quality level with Grok 4.5 — provided the "Opus-class" label holds up in practice.

18
00:02:19,912 --> 00:02:31,537
Critical voices: Musk's comparison to Opus 4.7 comes directly from xAI itself — independent benchmark results for Grok 4.5 were not yet available at time of writing.

19
00:02:31,537 --> 00:02:39,912
Open questions remain: how the price advantage plays out under real agentic workloads with high tool-call overhead is still unclear.

20
00:02:39,912 --> 00:02:52,937
Bottom Line: Grok 4.5 marks xAI's strategic shift away from the consumer chatbot toward a price war for enterprise and coding workloads — at the expense of Anthropic's and OpenAI's margins.

21
00:02:52,937 --> 00:02:54,275
More Top Stories.

22
00:02:54,275 --> 00:02:58,050
Beijing Weighs Export Restrictions on Chinese AI Models.

23
00:02:58,050 --> 00:03:12,500
China's Ministry of Commerce is consulting with Alibaba (Qwen), ByteDance (Doubao), and Z.ai (GLM-5.2) on restricting foreign access to the country's most advanced domestic AI models .

24
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:30,675
Under discussion: harsher penalties for leaks as violations of the National Security Law, plus a tiered control system — basic open-source tools subject to registration, advanced models requiring security review, and frontier models restricted to domestic use only or not released at all .

25
00:03:30,675 --> 00:03:38,987
Beijing is thus increasingly treating AI — much like the US does — as "a critical national asset that requires controls" .

26
00:03:38,987 --> 00:03:42,400
The timeline and final scope of the measures remain open .

27
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:43,225
So what?

28
00:03:43,225 --> 00:03:56,375
Should China actually restrict its open-weight models like Qwen or GLM, it would hit the Western open-source scene hard — Qwen derivatives in particular are currently among the most widely used base models outside the US.

29
00:03:56,375 --> 00:04:02,187
UN Global Dialogue on AI Governance: All 193 States at the Table.

30
00:04:02,187 --> 00:04:17,025
On July 6 and 7, the first session of the UN Global Dialogue on AI Governance took place in Geneva — with representatives from all 193 UN member states as well as industry, civil society, and academia .

31
00:04:17,025 --> 00:04:23,962
A 40-member international panel of experts released the first comprehensive global governance assessment during the session .

32
00:04:23,962 --> 00:04:34,087
Discussions covered opportunities such as productivity gains and improved healthcare systems, but also risks like disinformation and the erosion of democratic processes .

33
00:04:34,087 --> 00:04:38,300
"Science cannot guarantee that AI will not cause catastrophic harm."

34
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:39,675
— Yoshua Bengio

35
00:04:39,794 --> 00:04:50,444
Particularly notable: Ambassador Rein Tammsaar pointed to the geopolitical concentration of frontier AI development in just two countries — the US and China .

36
00:04:50,444 --> 00:04:51,269
So what?

37
00:04:51,269 --> 00:04:58,394
For every other state (and their companies), this means a structural dependence on models whose regulation they have no say in.

38
00:04:58,394 --> 00:05:03,856
AI Agent Startup Lets Its Own Agent Run $100M Funding Round.

39
00:05:03,856 --> 00:05:14,169
Jersey City-based enterprise agent startup Lyzr had its AI agent, "SivaClaw," run its own Series B funding round — as a living proof of product .

40
00:05:14,169 --> 00:05:23,744
The agent answered questions from over 130 investors, drafted investment memos, and even tracked which slides investors spent the most time viewing .

41
00:05:23,744 --> 00:05:36,806
The round closed at $100 million in Series B funding at roughly a $500 million valuation, with $400 million in total interest from Silicon Valley, the Middle East, and the financial sector .

42
00:05:36,806 --> 00:05:44,844
"There's so much capital chasing AI bets that startup founders with traction barely have to leave their desks to raise nine figures."

43
00:05:44,844 --> 00:05:45,669
So what?

44
00:05:45,669 --> 00:06:01,256
Whether this is a PR stunt or a genuine preview of agentic fundraising processes remains to be seen — but the sheer demand of $400 million against a $100 million target shows just how overheated the AI funding market remains.

45
00:06:01,256 --> 00:06:02,206
Quick Hits.

46
00:06:02,206 --> 00:06:03,281
Briefly noted:.

47
00:06:03,281 --> 00:06:22,406
Meta: New MTIA chip generation enters production in September to cut GPU costs amid an unprecedented component shortage — planned 2026 CapEx: $125-145B, 7 GW of compute capacity with planned doubling in 2027 .

48
00:06:22,406 --> 00:06:35,006
DeepSeek: Chinese company is developing its own inference chip to reduce dependence on Nvidia and Huawei — already ramping up hiring of chip design engineers and holding confidential foundry talks .

49
00:06:35,006 --> 00:06:49,056
Google: Now labels AI-generated ads in "My Ad Center" for Search, YouTube, and Discover — automatic for Google's own AI ad tools, manual for externally created ads, with no independent verification .

50
00:06:49,056 --> 00:07:01,544
Ollama: Raises $65M Series B led by Theory Ventures, bringing total funding to $88M with 8.9M monthly users and just 14 employees .

51
00:07:01,544 --> 00:07:17,419
Tech Layoffs: Oracle cuts 21,000 jobs (-13%) despite a $3.7B quarterly profit (+27%), explicitly citing "adoption and deployment of AI technologies" as the reason .

52
00:07:17,419 --> 00:07:18,544
Tool of the Week.

53
00:07:18,544 --> 00:07:22,319
Ollama — Local AI models made as easy as Docker.

54
00:07:22,319 --> 00:07:30,544
Ollama makes open-source LLMs run locally without users having to worry about CUDA versions, quantization, or model formats .

55
00:07:30,544 --> 00:07:43,331
Following its fresh $65M Series B, the tool now offers a cloud service with subscription tiers billed by GPU time rather than token limits, alongside the free desktop version .

56
00:07:43,331 --> 00:07:54,469
Particularly useful for developer teams who want to test models locally or avoid cloud APIs for privacy reasons: a model like Llama or Mistral is ready to run with a single  command.

57
00:07:54,469 --> 00:07:58,619
"Open models came out in 2023 but were very hard to use."

58
00:07:58,619 --> 00:08:00,869
— Jeff Morgan, CEO Ollama

59
00:08:00,869 --> 00:08:13,519
With 176,000 GitHub stars and adoption at 85% of Fortune 500 companies, Ollama has effectively established itself as the standard interface for local AI models .

60
00:08:13,519 --> 00:08:14,519
Link to tool

61
00:08:14,519 --> 00:08:15,669
Fail of the Week.

62
00:08:15,669 --> 00:08:19,919
"AI-related" as a layoff reason — despite record profits

63
00:08:19,919 --> 00:08:27,994
TechCrunch maintains a running list of major tech layoffs in 2026 where employers explicitly cited AI as the reason .

64
00:08:27,994 --> 00:08:36,956
Roughly 120,000 tech jobs have already been cut in 2026, with AI the most frequently cited reason for layoffs in May .

65
00:08:36,956 --> 00:08:42,194
The paradox: several affected companies reported record revenues in the same period.

66
00:08:42,194 --> 00:08:53,931
Oracle cut 21,000 jobs (-13% of its workforce over 12 months) while posting a $3.7B quarterly profit (+27%) .

67
00:08:53,931 --> 00:09:09,806
GitLab trimmed 350 jobs (-14%) despite $264M in quarterly revenue (+23% YoY), citing a demand for "100x growth" in agentic workloads as justification .

68
00:09:09,806 --> 00:09:26,756
Root cause: Companies are using "AI efficiency" narratives to justify cost cuts to investors, regardless of whether the actual operational driver is automation, shifting demand, or simply margin pressure — a blending of PR messaging and genuine technical cause.

69
00:09:26,858 --> 00:09:41,833
What we're learning: Anyone reading corporate "AI replaced X jobs" statements should check the quarterly numbers in parallel — often, the stated reason for layoffs says more about investor-relations strategy than about the actual degree of automation.

70
00:09:41,833 --> 00:09:42,970
Number of the Week.

71
00:09:42,970 --> 00:09:44,708
$3 Trillion

72
00:09:44,708 --> 00:09:55,558
That's how much revenue the AI industry needs to generate, according to analysts, to justify the $1.5 trillion in infrastructure CapEx planned for 2026 .

73
00:09:55,558 --> 00:10:09,420
For comparison: Anthropic currently generates roughly $60B in ARR, while OpenAI stood at $20B ARR as of November 2025 — together, well under a tenth of the required sum .

74
00:10:09,420 --> 00:10:16,145
The gap is widening further amid growing competition from cheaper open-source models and falling token prices .

75
00:10:16,145 --> 00:10:24,483
"The required revenue per GW of capital expenditure has risen sharply due to these bottlenecks and rising construction costs."

76
00:10:24,483 --> 00:10:26,658
— David Cahn, Sequoia Capital

77
00:10:26,658 --> 00:10:34,320
Apollo chief economist Torsten Slok warns of recession risk should hyperscalers miss their 2028 profit projections .

